+## T6 — block/pin contact
+
+Status: in progress. Hard start 2026-11-13 19:16:41 UTC (13:16:41 CST); hard stop 19:56:41 UTC. Model: separate PETG block (40 x 30 x 20 mm, Ø10.2 through bore along Y), centered Ø10 x 50 mm steel pin, and two 1 mm long radius-1 mm soft pads at its ends to prevent free axial drift. The block bottom and pads' outer faces are fixed; the pin is pulled +Z by 250 N at each end. The only block/pin interface is a 0.1 mm radial-clearance no-penetration contact. Hand projected bearing stress is 500/(10 x 30) = 1.667 MPa. First mesh attempt: predicted 19,111 nodes, actual 24,034 (within 2x); maximum displacement was 0.825 mm. I had compared the whole model's maximum against a 0.15 mm pin-clearance estimate, which omitted block compliance, so I discarded the study before using any stress result. Revised hand estimate treats the 4.9 mm block roof as a simply supported 40 mm strip, 30 mm wide: I=30*4.9^3/12=294.1 mm^4; δ=500*40^3/(48*1900*294.1)=1.193 mm. Adding the 0.1 mm radial clearance gives about 1.29 mm pin rise. This is a conservative strip idealization; the retry logs the maximum displacement location to check it. The retry reuses the original hard deadline. Initial prelaunch status showed no HALF-CLOSED instance and 20.39 GB free; after the first owned job closed, the next prelaunch check showed only healthy answering instances and 19.84 GB free.
+
+The contact proof will use 0.5 N in the working direction and a bonded twin. The detailed 05-fea clearance-fit instruction says not to reverse a load when the body starts with clearance; it prescribes the working-direction proof. The generalized line in 07-done says reverse a tiny load for contact. I follow the clearance-specific instruction, since reversing would pull the centered pin away from either wall and test its stabilizer instead of seating contact. Studies must stay below 200,000 nodes and within the 40 minute limit.
+
+What could not be verified: pending.
+
+Elapsed: in progress.
+
+## T7 — diagnosis
+
+Status: pending.
+
+## Playbook/helper findings
+
+Findings so far: 07-done requires checking the expected node count before judging stresses, but swfea.static() only has a maximum-node guard and proceeds to solve/read results before the calling script can check its prediction. I set max_nodes to 2x the current predicted count to stop an oversized mesh before the solve; the helper still has no lower-bound pre-solve hook. The first mismatch exceeded the prediction by only 2.014x; the result was discarded. The 07-done maximum-displacement gate also comes after static() has already read arrays, so the helper does not expose a pre-stress result check. Do not treat either discarded run as a reported stress result. T4's local edge mesh control stayed at 1 mm across global refinements, so the exact sharp edge did not receive a genuine refinement series; that gap was exposed by offline node extraction and is why T4 remains partial. T6's contact-proof wording conflicts between 05-fea's specific clearance-fit case and 07-done's generalized reverse-load sentence; I record and follow the former for the centered clearance pin.
+
+## Final review
+
+End: pending.
