## T6 â€” block/pin contact

Status: in progress. Hard start 2026-11-13 19:16:41 UTC (13:16:41 CST); hard stop 19:56:41 UTC. Model: separate PETG block (40 x 30 x 20 mm, Ã˜10.2 through bore along Y), centered Ã˜10 x 50 mm steel pin, and two 1 mm long radius-1 mm soft pads at its ends to prevent free axial drift. The block bottom and pads' outer faces are fixed; the pin is pulled +Z by 250 N at each end. The only block/pin interface is a 0.1 mm radial-clearance no-penetration contact. Hand projected bearing stress is 500/(10 x 30) = 1.667 MPa. First mesh attempt: predicted 19,111 nodes, actual 24,034 (within 2x); maximum displacement was 0.825 mm. I had compared the whole model's maximum against a 0.15 mm pin-clearance estimate, which omitted block compliance, so I discarded the study before using any stress result. Revised hand estimate treats the 4.9 mm block roof as a simply supported 40 mm strip, 30 mm wide: I=30*4.9^3/12=294.1 mm^4; Î´=500*40^3/(48*1900*294.1)=1.193 mm. Adding the 0.1 mm radial clearance gives about 1.29 mm pin rise. This is a conservative strip idealization; the retry logs the maximum displacement location to check it. The retry reuses the original hard deadline. Initial prelaunch status showed no HALF-CLOSED instance and 20.39 GB free; after the first owned job closed, the next prelaunch check showed only healthy answering instances and 19.84 GB free.

The contact proof will use 0.5 N in the working direction and a bonded twin. The detailed 05-fea clearance-fit instruction says not to reverse a load when the body starts with clearance; it prescribes the working-direction proof. The generalized line in 07-done says reverse a tiny load for contact. I follow the clearance-specific instruction, since reversing would pull the centered pin away from either wall and test its stabilizer instead of seating contact. Studies must stay below 200,000 nodes and within the 40 minute limit.

